Yes—though I’d soften “everyone” to a recurring human tension: people often want the vitality associated with vice without surrendering the identity, relationships, and values associated with virtue.

That is why simple suppression often fails. The underlying drive may contain something useful:

aggression → courage / competition / protection
lust → eroticism / intimacy / play
pride → dignity / mastery / ambition
greed → acquisition / building / provision
deception → imagination / performance / surprise
risk → exploration / adventure

The GoalSeek problem becomes: preserve the energy, change the expression, constrain the externalities.

So rather than:

virtue = amputate vice

you can search for:

impulse → underlying need/energy → consensual bounded expression → consequence check → integration

A competitive person gets sport. Someone who enjoys danger gets controlled adventure. Someone who likes playing identities gets theater or role-play. Someone with strong erotic imagination gets a mutually chosen space for it. Someone who enjoys strategic misdirection gets chess, games, fiction, negotiation with understood rules. The drive doesn’t have to disappear for the person to remain aligned with their values.

And there is another important distinction:

having access to a vice is different from being governed by it.

Integration means, “I know this capacity exists in me; I can call on pieces of it deliberately; it doesn’t get automatic command authority.”

That is stronger than maintaining an outwardly virtuous facade while secretly doing whatever one wants. In that case the virtue exists mainly as reputation management. The harder and more interesting solution is internal consistency with a larger behavioral vocabulary.

So our GoalSeek might be:

Find the largest space in which a person can express their full range—wildness, appetite, competition, sexuality, ambition, anger, play—while preserving consent, dignity, responsibility, and the ability of everyone involved to leave the game.

Then virtue stops meaning sterility.

It becomes power under chosen governance.

And that fits the whole thread we’ve been building: don’t kill the thorn, fire, storm, child, competitor, trickster, or animal automatically. Determine its function, give it an appropriate arena, and retain control over when it gets the wheel.